{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1290,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s plenty of stuff in the universe\nthat defies the human urge to categorize everything:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5060,
    "dDurationMs": 2449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, is Pluto a planet?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7509,
    "dDurationMs": 2581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is this a new species or subspecies?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10090,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Man or woman?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11630,
    "dDurationMs": 1799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Green or blue or teal?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13429,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, sometimes nature gives us a helping\nhand and provides a hard, physical line distinguishing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17830,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two things, like the line between white dwarf\nstars, neutron stars, and black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22920,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the different possible kinds of\ncorpses of dead stars, and what distinguishes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26600,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them is how they hold themselves up against\nthe inexorable attraction of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30640,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s begin, though, with what makes something\na star in the first place: fusion of hydrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35590,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Below about 8% of the mass of the sun, a gas\nball isn’t hot and high pressure enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39950,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for hydrogen to fuse and you get a failed\nstar , essentially a really heavy version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43840,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Jupiter or Saturn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45420,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Above this dividing line are stars like red\ndwarfs, our sun, and so on , which use hydrogen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50710,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fusion to keep themselves hot enough to stave\noff gravitational collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54020,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But eventually stars run out of fuel for nuclear\nfusion, and die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57860,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when a star dies, it will turn into one\nof three very different and physically-distinct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61650,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things . If the star is small its core will\nturn into a white dwarf, which is an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66150,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the size of the earth, almost as massive\nas the sun, and is held up against gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70580,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not by heat but by electrons repelling each\nother due to the “Pauli exclusion principle”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75039,
    "dDurationMs": 3661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". This effect is essentially a star-sized\nversion of how electrons fill subsequently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78700,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bigger orbitals around an atom, because of\na fundamental law of the universe that no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82130,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two electrons can share the exact same state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84540,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the star is bigger , its core will be too\nheavy for this kind of electron repulsion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88860,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to hold it up against gravity, and instead\nit’ll collapse even more until it’s around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92890,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 kilometers in size , all of its electrons\nand protons fuse together into neutrons, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96760,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s held up instead by the nuclear repulsion\nbetween neutrons, as well as the neutron version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101280,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the pauli exclusion principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102690,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aka, a neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105170,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the dying star is yet bigger still (or\nif two neutron stars collide and merge), its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109790,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core will be too heavy for either the strong\nnuclear force or pauli exclusion principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to hold it up against gravity, and it will\ncollapse until it’s held up by – well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118120,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as far as we know there’s not anything left\nto hold up stuff this dense , and it’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122130,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse into a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123520,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when a star dies, what it turns into depends\non its mass, and what distinguishes these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128390,
    "dDurationMs": 4069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star corpses from each other is what they’re\nmade of and how they hold themselves up against"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132459,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity (or don’t)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135989,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But enough about space objects that have nice,\nclean, easy definitions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139319,
    "dDurationMs": 2691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's also a lot of stuff out there that\ndoesn't fit nicely into boxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142010,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kate (interjects): Like moons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143810,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We supposedly have a good definition for what\na moon is but our solar system is full of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149400,
    "dDurationMs": 4789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects that fall into a weird grey area between\nmoon and not moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154189,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Come over to MinuteEarth to find out why our\ndefinition of \"moon\" might be broken!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
